kipedia.org/wiki/Mediana)** — Mediana is an important archaeological site from the late Roman period in the eastern suburb of Niš. It was a luxurious imperial villa complex associated with **Constantine the Great**, who was born in Naissus.\n- **[Mediana: The Roman Ruins of Niš – Leighton Travels](https://leightontravels.com/2024/11/03/mediana-the-roman-ruins-of-nis)** — The site contains the remains of a grand palace, forum, church, thermal baths, and storage buildings, sheltered under a modern protective structure.\n- **[Mediana Archaeological Site – Panacomp](https://www.panacomp.net/mediana-archaeological-site)** — The site is located on the road from Niš toward Niška Banja (spa), covering 40 hectares near the Nišava River and thermal springs.\n\n### Naissus Background\n- **[Naissus – EBSCO Research Starters](https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/naissus)** — Established by the Romans in the 1st century AD, Naissus rose to prominence due to its strategic position on the **Via Militaris** military and trade route.\n- **[History of Niš – Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Ni%C5%A1)** — Niš was a key Roman town on the Via Militaris and is notable as the birthplace of Constantine the Great.\n\n### Note on \"Oblik\"\nThe search results didn't directly surface content about a specific spring called \"Oblik\" feeding the Niš aqueduct — results for that term mostly returned unrelated Serbian-language pages (the word \"oblik\" means \"shape/form\" in Serbian, which diluted the results). If \"Oblik\" refers to the specific water source for the Naissus/Mediana aqueduct, it may be documented in specialized archaeological literature (e.g., Starinar journal or National Museum of Niš publications) rather than easily accessible web pages. Would you like me to search with different terms to dig deeper into that aspect?\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"cal