tepped drops (gradus). For lead fistulae under pressure, steep gradient gives high pressure head: 10 m head at villa → pressure ~1 bar per 10 m. Fontane need 1–3 m head for a modest jet; a 10 m head gives plenty. Compute flow: lead pipe diameters: standard Roman fistulae ~ digitus 2.31 cm; common diameters 2.5–7 cm? Actually Roman lead pipes: diameters 2.5–30 cm; Caladrius inscription list: quinaria (5 digit? no...). Frontinus' quinaria ~ 2.3 cm nominal. For Mediana: lead pipe found in room 12 (sonda 107) — diameter? Not recorded in base maybe. The nimfej lead pipes visible in photo. Let me compute for a fistula of internal diameter 5 cm on a 2% gradient.\n\nHydraulic computation: For lead pipes laid on the aqueduct channel bed, they often ran full (pressurized) or as inverted siphons. With 2% slope, using Hazen-Williams or Manning for a pipe flowing full: Manning's n for lead ~0.011 (smooth). Pipe D = 0.05 m, A = 1.9635e-3 m², R = D/4 = 0.0125 m. S = 0.02. V = (1/n) R^(2/3) S^(1/2) = (1/0.011) * 0.0125^(2/3) * 0.1414. 0.0125^(2/3): ln(0.0125) = -4.382; *2/3 = -2.921; e^-2.921 = 0.0539. So V = 90.9 * 0.0539 * 0.1414 = 0.6927 m/s. Q = 0.6927*1.9635e-3 = 1.36e-3 m³/s ≈ 1.4 l/s. For D=7 cm: A=3.848e-3, R=0.0175, R^(2/3): ln 0.0175 = -4.045, *2/3 = -2.697, e = 0.0674. V = 90.9*0.0674*0.1414 = 0.866. Q = 3.33e-3 = 3.3 l/s.\n\nAlternatively use frontinus: quinaria Q estimate ~ 0.44 l/s? Frontinus quinaria cross-section based estimates: 1 quinaria ≈ 4.5–5 m³/day? Hmm. Let me be careful. Frontinus quinaria = pipe with circumference 5¼ digits? A quinaria has opening of 1¼ digits diameter ≈ 2.31 cm? Estimates: quinaria ≈ 0.48 l/s under normal head? Various reconstructions: 1 quinaria ≈ 18,000–19,000 modii/day? Hmm, let me not overreach. I can cite Hodge's estimate: quinaria delivers about 0.44 l/s under 10 ft head? Something like: \"one quinaria supplied about 20 m³/day\" — roughly 0.23 l/s. Hodge: Roman domestic supply 500 l/day per person? Let's just do the Manning calc tra